<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<TcPlcObject Version="1.1.0.1" ProductVersion="3.1.4026.18">
  <POU Name="FB_ContainerMover" Id="{5e4501d0-0036-4d19-9a41-000000000001}" SpecialFunc="None">
    <Declaration><![CDATA[(*
    The container mover equipment module: one shuttle, its two axes and its
    manipulator, taking a container between the aisle end and a storage
    location.

    There is one of these per aisle. The rail is horizontal along the aisle,
    the mast is vertical, and the manipulator on the deck reaches into the rack
    on either side of the rail - which is why an order names a side and why the
    hook reach is signed.

    THE STATE MODEL, in the order it walks:

      IDLE             powered, no order, position not to be trusted;
      HOMING           driving both axes to the home reference;
      MOVE_HORIZONTAL  travelling, with the rail the axis still outstanding;
      MOVE_VERTICAL    travelling, with the mast the axis still outstanding;
      HOOKS_ENGAGING   hooks reaching into the rack and latching a container;
      LIFTING          the cylinder stroke that transfers the container's
                       weight - up off the shelf for a retrieval, down onto it
                       for a store;
      HOOKS_RELEASING  hooks letting go and coming home clear of the racking;
      AT_TARGET        order complete, resting where it finished;
      FAULT            latched, and going nowhere until it is acknowledged.

    THE MOVE IS COORDINATED, NOT SEQUENTIAL. Both axes are given their targets
    on the same scan and travel at the same time; MOVE_HORIZONTAL and
    MOVE_VERTICAL are which axis is still the laggard, not two moves one after
    the other. A shuttle that drove its rail and then its mast would take the
    sum of the two legs on every order instead of the longer of them, which on
    a machine this size is most of a shift.

    THE LOAD TRANSFER IS INTERLOCKED. Three actuators cross into the racking
    and none of them may move while another is out there: the axes may not
    travel unless the hooks report themselves home, and the hooks may not reach
    unless both axes report themselves in position and the cylinder is at the
    end its stroke starts from. Every one of those is a sensor, not a timer,
    because the thing being guarded against is a hook that did not come back.

    THE MAP IS THE AUTHORITY on what is in a location, and this block does not
    keep a second copy. An order is checked against it before the shuttle
    moves, and the map is told what happened only once the container has
    actually changed hands - a location marked occupied by an order that was
    still in flight is a location the next allocation will refuse for a
    container that never arrived.
*)
FUNCTION_BLOCK FB_ContainerMover
VAR_INPUT
	// The rail. Commanded every scan, never pulsed.
	railAxis : I_PositioningAxis;
	// The mast.
	liftAxis : I_PositioningAxis;
	// The hooks are latched onto a container.
	HooksEngaged : BOOL;
	// The hooks are home and clear of the racking. Deliberately its own sensor
	// rather than NOT HooksEngaged: half way out both are false, and that is
	// exactly the position in which the axes must not be allowed to travel.
	HooksReleased : BOOL;
	// The lifting cylinder is at the top of its stroke - a container on the
	// hooks is clear of the shelf it came off.
	CylinderRaised : BOOL;
	// The lifting cylinder is at the bottom of its stroke - a container on the
	// hooks is resting on the shelf.
	CylinderLowered : BOOL;
	Control : ST_MoverControl;
	// The PackML Reset. Acknowledges the drives and releases the fault latch -
	// the single point at which a tripped shuttle becomes orderable again.
	Reset : BOOL;
END_VAR
VAR_IN_OUT
	// The warehouse's own picture of itself, shared with everything else that
	// reads or writes it. IN_OUT rather than a copy for the obvious reason: a
	// shuttle that stored a container into its own private map would leave the
	// real one saying the location was still waiting for it.
	locationMap : FB_StorageLocationMap;
END_VAR
VAR_OUTPUT
	CurrentState : E_MoverState;
	// The axes have been driven to a known reference since the last fault.
	Homed : BOOL;
	// An order is in flight. Not the same as "not at target": a shuttle that
	// has finished is resting, and a shuttle that has faulted is not working.
	Busy : BOOL;
	Fault : BOOL;
	// Why the shuttle faulted. Zero while it has not. The first cause to
	// arrive is the one kept, so a timeout that then trips a drive still
	// reports the timeout - the drive tripped because the move was already
	// wrong, not the other way round.
	ErrorID : UDINT;
	// The flat index of the location the order named, so a caller reading this
	// block alone can say which location a fault was about.
	TargetIndex : UINT;
	RailTarget : LREAL;
	LiftTarget : LREAL;
	// How far the hooks must reach, and which way. Negative is the near rack
	// face of the aisle, positive the far one.
	HookReach : LREAL;
	// The travel left on the LONGER of the two axes, in mm. The distance a
	// coordinated move still has to run, which is not the sum of the two legs.
	TravelRemaining : LREAL;
	EngageHooks : BOOL;
	// The end of its stroke the lifting cylinder is being held at. Set on the
	// approach to whichever end the order arrives in - raised for a store,
	// because the container is on the deck being carried above shelf height,
	// lowered for a retrieval, because the hooks have to come in under one
	// already on a shelf - and flipped exactly once, when the container changes
	// hands. It is never released afterwards: the cylinder is what is holding
	// whatever the hooks have.
	RaiseCylinder : BOOL;
END_VAR
VAR
	order : E_MoveOrder;
	orderBarcode : STRING(32);
	orderStoredAt : DATE_AND_TIME;
	railDemand : LREAL;
	liftDemand : LREAL;
	railRemaining : LREAL;
	liftRemaining : LREAL;
	faultRequest : BOOL;
	moveTimer : TON;
	faultLatch : RS;
END_VAR
VAR CONSTANT
	// The shuttle stood in one move for longer than the recipe allows.
	ERROR_MOVE_TIMEOUT : UDINT := 16#85010001;
	// The order named a flat index the racking does not have.
	ERROR_TARGET_NOT_IN_RACKING : UDINT := 16#85010002;
	// The order named a location in an aisle this shuttle cannot reach.
	ERROR_TARGET_IN_ANOTHER_AISLE : UDINT := 16#85010003;
	// A store order for a location nobody set aside for the container.
	ERROR_TARGET_NOT_RESERVED : UDINT := 16#85010004;
	// A retrieval order for a location the map says holds nothing.
	ERROR_TARGET_NOT_OCCUPIED : UDINT := 16#85010005;
	// The surveyed position of the target is outside the travel the shuttle
	// physically has.
	ERROR_TARGET_OUTSIDE_ENVELOPE : UDINT := 16#85010006;
	ERROR_AXIS_FAULT : UDINT := 16#85010007;
END_VAR
]]></Declaration>
    <Implementation>
      <ST><![CDATA[// Drive
// Power is re-requested every scan rather than once, so a drive that comes up
// late - or comes back after a trip has been acknowledged - is live again
// without needing a fresh order.
railAxis.Enable();
liftAxis.Enable();

IF Reset THEN
	railAxis.Reset();
	liftAxis.Reset();
END_IF

// Fault
// One allowance over the whole move. IsMoving is the set of states in which
// the shuttle owes somebody a container, so the timer is armed by having work
// outstanding rather than by any one actuator being commanded - a sequence
// stuck waiting on a hook sensor is just as overdue as one stuck on an axis.
moveTimer(IN := IsMoving(), PT := Control.MoveTimeout);

IF moveTimer.Q THEN
	RaiseFault(Code := ERROR_MOVE_TIMEOUT);
END_IF

IF railAxis.Error OR liftAxis.Error THEN
	RaiseFault(Code := ERROR_AXIS_FAULT);
END_IF

// Reset-dominant, so a drive stuck reporting a fault cannot make the module
// permanently unclearable - it re-latches on the next scan through the axis,
// which is the drive's answer rather than the latch's.
faultLatch(SET := faultRequest, RESET1 := Reset);
faultRequest := FALSE;
Fault := faultLatch.Q1;

IF NOT Fault THEN
	ErrorID := 0;
END_IF

// Statemachine
IF Fault THEN
	IF CurrentState <> E_MoverState.FAULT THEN
		// The order dies with the fault rather than resuming on acknowledgement:
		// whatever the shuttle was carrying has to be found by a person before
		// the machine is told what to do with it. Homed goes with it because
		// the shuttle cannot know what moved while it was faulted, and re-homing
		// is cheap next to reaching into a rack from the wrong level.
		order := E_MoveOrder.NONE;
		Homed := FALSE;
		CurrentState := E_MoverState.FAULT;
	END_IF
ELSE
	CASE CurrentState OF
		E_MoverState.IDLE:
			IF order <> E_MoveOrder.NONE THEN
				CurrentState := E_MoverState.HOMING;
			END_IF

		E_MoverState.HOMING:
			IF railAxis.InPosition AND liftAxis.InPosition THEN
				Homed := TRUE;
				IF order <> E_MoveOrder.NONE THEN
					CurrentState := E_MoverState.MOVE_HORIZONTAL;
				ELSE
					CurrentState := E_MoverState.IDLE;
				END_IF
			END_IF

		E_MoverState.MOVE_HORIZONTAL:
			IF railAxis.InPosition THEN
				CurrentState := E_MoverState.MOVE_VERTICAL;
			END_IF

		E_MoverState.MOVE_VERTICAL:
			// The interlock sits on the way OUT of the travel rather than in a
			// state of its own, because it is one question - is anything of this
			// shuttle still out in the racking, and is the cylinder set for the
			// transfer it is about to make - and the answer has to be yes before
			// the hooks are allowed to move at all.
			IF liftAxis.InPosition AND HooksReleased AND CylinderAtCommandedEnd() THEN
				CurrentState := E_MoverState.HOOKS_ENGAGING;
			END_IF

		E_MoverState.HOOKS_ENGAGING:
			IF HooksEngaged THEN
				// The one flip in the whole order: the cylinder now strokes the
				// other way, and that stroke IS the transfer - up for a
				// retrieval, taking the container off its shelf, down for a
				// store, setting it onto one.
				RaiseCylinder := order = E_MoveOrder.RETRIEVE;
				CurrentState := E_MoverState.LIFTING;
			END_IF

		E_MoverState.LIFTING:
			IF CylinderAtCommandedEnd() THEN
				CurrentState := E_MoverState.HOOKS_RELEASING;
			END_IF

		E_MoverState.HOOKS_RELEASING:
			// The map is told the container has arrived on the scan the hooks
			// come home, and not before: everything up to here is a container
			// still in the shuttle's hands.
			IF HooksReleased THEN
				CompleteOrder();
				CurrentState := E_MoverState.AT_TARGET;
			END_IF

		E_MoverState.AT_TARGET:
			// A resting state, not a pulse. The next order goes straight to
			// travel - the shuttle is already homed and standing at a location.
			IF order <> E_MoveOrder.NONE THEN
				CurrentState := E_MoverState.MOVE_HORIZONTAL;
			END_IF

		E_MoverState.FAULT:
			// Only reached with the latch already clear, since a standing fault
			// is handled above the CASE. Back to IDLE rather than to AT_TARGET,
			// because an acknowledged shuttle has no order and no reference: the
			// next order re-homes it before it goes anywhere.
			CurrentState := E_MoverState.IDLE;
	END_CASE
END_IF

// Feedback
IF CurrentState = E_MoverState.HOMING THEN
	railDemand := Control.PositionReferences.HomeRail;
	liftDemand := Control.PositionReferences.HomeLift;
ELSE
	railDemand := RailTarget;
	liftDemand := LiftTarget;
END_IF

// Both axes get their demand on the same scan, which is what makes the move
// coordinated: neither waits for the other to finish its leg.
railAxis.MoveAbsolute(Position := railDemand);
liftAxis.MoveAbsolute(Position := liftDemand);

EngageHooks := CurrentState = E_MoverState.HOOKS_ENGAGING
	OR CurrentState = E_MoverState.LIFTING;

// Status
railRemaining := railDemand - railAxis.ActualPosition;
IF railRemaining < 0.0 THEN
	railRemaining := 0.0 - railRemaining;
END_IF

liftRemaining := liftDemand - liftAxis.ActualPosition;
IF liftRemaining < 0.0 THEN
	liftRemaining := 0.0 - liftRemaining;
END_IF

// The longer leg, not the sum: the axes run at the same time, so the move is
// over when the one with furthest to go arrives.
IF railRemaining > liftRemaining THEN
	TravelRemaining := railRemaining;
ELSE
	TravelRemaining := liftRemaining;
END_IF

Busy := IsMoving();]]></ST>
    </Implementation>
    <Method Name="IsMoving" Id="{5e4501d0-0036-4d19-9a41-000000000002}">
      <Declaration><![CDATA[// The shuttle owes somebody a container: an order has been accepted and has
// not finished. IDLE, AT_TARGET and FAULT are all places it is standing with
// nothing outstanding, which is the same set the move timeout must not run in.
METHOD PRIVATE IsMoving : BOOL]]></Declaration>
      <Implementation>
        <ST><![CDATA[IsMoving := CurrentState = E_MoverState.HOMING
	OR CurrentState = E_MoverState.MOVE_HORIZONTAL
	OR CurrentState = E_MoverState.MOVE_VERTICAL
	OR CurrentState = E_MoverState.HOOKS_ENGAGING
	OR CurrentState = E_MoverState.LIFTING
	OR CurrentState = E_MoverState.HOOKS_RELEASING;]]></ST>
      </Implementation>
    </Method>
    <Method Name="CylinderAtCommandedEnd" Id="{5e4501d0-0036-4d19-9a41-000000000003}">
      <Declaration><![CDATA[// The lifting cylinder has reached the end of its stroke that it is currently
// being held at.
//
// Asked twice for two different reasons - before the hooks reach into the
// rack, and again to decide the transfer has completed - and it is one method
// because it is one question. A cylinder that has not got there is not a
// fault; it is a stroke that has not finished, and the move timeout is what
// turns it into one if it never does.
METHOD PRIVATE CylinderAtCommandedEnd : BOOL]]></Declaration>
      <Implementation>
        <ST><![CDATA[IF RaiseCylinder THEN
	CylinderAtCommandedEnd := CylinderRaised;
ELSE
	CylinderAtCommandedEnd := CylinderLowered;
END_IF]]></ST>
      </Implementation>
    </Method>
    <Method Name="RaiseFault" Id="{5e4501d0-0036-4d19-9a41-000000000004}">
      <Declaration><![CDATA[// Asks for the fault latch to be set on the next pass through the body, and
// records why.
//
// A request rather than a direct write to the latch, because this is called
// from order methods that run between scans as well as from the body: the
// latch is driven in exactly one place so that Reset stays dominant over every
// cause, however it arrived.
METHOD PRIVATE RaiseFault
VAR_INPUT
	Code : UDINT;
END_VAR
]]></Declaration>
      <Implementation>
        <ST><![CDATA[// First cause wins. A shuttle that timed out and then tripped its drive on the
// way to a stop must report the timeout: the drive tripped because the move had
// already gone wrong, and reporting it would send a technician to the amplifier.
IF faultRequest OR faultLatch.Q1 THEN
	RETURN;
END_IF

faultRequest := TRUE;
ErrorID := Code;]]></ST>
      </Implementation>
    </Method>
    <Method Name="StoreContainer" Id="{5e4501d0-0036-4d19-9a41-000000000005}">
      <Declaration><![CDATA[// Take the container on the deck to the location the MES assigned it. FALSE
// if the order was not accepted - either refused because the shuttle is busy,
// or faulted because the order and the map disagree about the warehouse.
//
// The barcode and the timestamp travel with the order rather than being read
// here, because they are the container's and the shuttle never sees it: the
// registration station scanned it, and the map is told both only once the
// container is actually in the rack.
METHOD StoreContainer : BOOL
VAR_INPUT
	Index : UINT;
	Barcode : STRING(32);
	StoredAt : DATE_AND_TIME;
END_VAR
]]></Declaration>
      <Implementation>
        <ST><![CDATA[StoreContainer := AcceptOrder(Kind := E_MoveOrder.STORE, Index := Index);

IF StoreContainer THEN
	orderBarcode := Barcode;
	orderStoredAt := StoredAt;
END_IF]]></ST>
      </Implementation>
    </Method>
    <Method Name="RetrieveContainer" Id="{5e4501d0-0036-4d19-9a41-000000000006}">
      <Declaration><![CDATA[// Fetch the container in a location back to the shuttle deck. FALSE if the
// order was not accepted.
METHOD RetrieveContainer : BOOL
VAR_INPUT
	Index : UINT;
END_VAR
]]></Declaration>
      <Implementation>
        <ST><![CDATA[RetrieveContainer := AcceptOrder(Kind := E_MoveOrder.RETRIEVE, Index := Index);]]></ST>
      </Implementation>
    </Method>
    <Method Name="AcceptOrder" Id="{5e4501d0-0036-4d19-9a41-000000000007}">
      <Declaration><![CDATA[// Checks an order against the map and the racking, and turns the location it
// names into the two axis targets the sequencer drives to.
//
// Every check happens BEFORE the shuttle moves, and that is the point of doing
// it here rather than in the state machine: a target the machine cannot serve
// costs a refusal, not a trip into the racking that has to be aborted halfway.
//
// The division of outcomes is deliberate. A busy shuttle REFUSES - an order
// arriving while one is in flight is ordinary flow control and the unit above
// re-offers it. Everything else FAULTS, because each one means the MES and the
// PLC disagree about the warehouse, and a machine that silently declined those
// would keep taking orders while the disagreement got worse.
METHOD PRIVATE AcceptOrder : BOOL
VAR_INPUT
	Kind : E_MoveOrder;
	Index : UINT;
END_VAR
VAR
	aisle : UINT;
	side : UINT;
	level : UINT;
	position : UINT;
	rail : LREAL;
	lift : LREAL;
END_VAR
]]></Declaration>
      <Implementation>
        <ST><![CDATA[AcceptOrder := FALSE;

IF Fault OR faultRequest OR order <> E_MoveOrder.NONE THEN
	RETURN;
END_IF

IF NOT locationMap.AddressOf(FlatIndex := Index,
                             Aisle => aisle,
                             Side => side,
                             Level => level,
                             Position => position) THEN
	RaiseFault(Code := ERROR_TARGET_NOT_IN_RACKING);
	RETURN;
END_IF

IF aisle <> Control.AisleServed THEN
	RaiseFault(Code := ERROR_TARGET_IN_ANOTHER_AISLE);
	RETURN;
END_IF

// The two orders ask the map for opposite answers, and both are interlocks
// rather than sanity checks: storing into a location nobody set aside means
// the next allocation can hand that slot out from under the container, and
// retrieving from an empty one sends the shuttle after nothing while the
// container it was meant to fetch stays where it is.
IF Kind = E_MoveOrder.STORE AND locationMap.StateAt(Index := Index) <> E_LocationState.RESERVED THEN
	RaiseFault(Code := ERROR_TARGET_NOT_RESERVED);
	RETURN;
END_IF

IF Kind = E_MoveOrder.RETRIEVE AND locationMap.StateAt(Index := Index) <> E_LocationState.OCCUPIED THEN
	RaiseFault(Code := ERROR_TARGET_NOT_OCCUPIED);
	RETURN;
END_IF

rail := Control.PositionReferences.StoragePositions[position];
lift := Control.PositionReferences.LevelHeights[level];

// A surveyed position outside the travel the shuttle has is a rack model and a
// machine that no longer describe each other - a rack extended without the
// table being re-surveyed, or the other way round. Refusing here is what stops
// the axis being commanded to drive into its own end stop.
IF rail < Control.PositionReferences.RailMinimum
	OR rail > Control.PositionReferences.RailMaximum
	OR lift < Control.PositionReferences.LiftMinimum
	OR lift > Control.PositionReferences.LiftMaximum THEN
	RaiseFault(Code := ERROR_TARGET_OUTSIDE_ENVELOPE);
	RETURN;
END_IF

order := Kind;
TargetIndex := Index;
RailTarget := rail;
LiftTarget := lift;

// The approach stroke, which is the one the order arrives in: a store is
// carrying its container on raised hooks, a retrieval is going to fetch one
// off a shelf and must come in under it.
RaiseCylinder := Kind = E_MoveOrder.STORE;

// The near face of the aisle is reached the other way from the far one, and
// the sign is the whole of that difference - the reach itself is the same.
IF side = 1 THEN
	HookReach := 0.0 - Control.PositionReferences.SideReach;
ELSE
	HookReach := Control.PositionReferences.SideReach;
END_IF

AcceptOrder := TRUE;]]></ST>
      </Implementation>
    </Method>
    <Method Name="CompleteOrder" Id="{5e4501d0-0036-4d19-9a41-000000000008}">
      <Declaration><![CDATA[// Tells the map what the shuttle just did, and clears the order.
//
// This is the only place the map is written, and it runs on the scan the hooks
// come home - the first moment at which the container is somewhere it will
// stay if the machine stops.
METHOD PRIVATE CompleteOrder]]></Declaration>
      <Implementation>
        <ST><![CDATA[IF order = E_MoveOrder.STORE THEN
	locationMap.Store(Index := TargetIndex, Barcode := orderBarcode, StoredAt := orderStoredAt);
ELSIF order = E_MoveOrder.RETRIEVE THEN
	// A retrieved container is on the deck and out of the warehouse's
	// inventory, so the location goes back to the free pool with the
	// container's details cleared off it.
	locationMap.Release(Index := TargetIndex);
END_IF

order := E_MoveOrder.NONE;]]></ST>
      </Implementation>
    </Method>
  </POU>
</TcPlcObject>
